              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "The \"G-Wagon\" (officially named the **Mercedes-Benz G-Class**) is a **luxury mid-size SUV (Sport Utility Vehicle)** known for its iconic boxy styling and extreme off-road capabilities. \n\nThe vehicle has a unique history and set of characteristics that define what type of car it is:\n\n### 1. The Name Origin\n\"G-Wagon\" (often spelled *G-Wagen*) is short for **Gel\u00e4ndewagen**, which is German for **\"terrain vehicle\"** or **\"off-road vehicle.\"** \n\n### 2. Design and Construction\n* **Boxy, Military-Inspired Styling:** Originally developed in the late 1970s as a military vehicle, the G-Class has retained its upright, boxy, and rugged silhouette for decades. Features like its external door hinges, flat glass, and spare tire mounted on the back door are signature elements of its design.\n* **Body-on-Frame Build:** Unlike most modern SUVs, which use \"unibody\" construction (like typical passenger cars), the G-Wagon is built using a traditional **body-on-frame** method (like a pickup truck). This makes the vehicle incredibly heavy, durable, and highly resistant to twisting when driving over rough terrain.\n\n### 3. Ultimate Off-Road Capability\nThough most modern buyers drive them in suburban or city environments, the G-Wagon is a highly capable, military-grade off-roader. It features standard **four-wheel drive (4WD)** and is famous for having **three fully locking differentials** (front, center, and rear), allowing it to climb extreme inclines and navigate mud, rocks, and deep water with ease.\n\n### 4. Transition to a Luxury Status Symbol\nWhile it started as a utilitarian workhorse, Mercedes-Benz has spent decades refining the G-Wagon into a **high-end luxury flagship**. It features hand-crafted luxury leather interiors, cutting-edge technology, and high-performance engines. \n* **The AMG Versions:** The most popular models are often the high-performance variants tuned by Mercedes' AMG division (such as the **AMG G 63**), which feature powerful twin-turbo V8 engines, side-exit exhausts, and sports suspension.\n* **Electric Version:** There is also a fully electric version of the vehicle, called the **G 580 with EQ Technology** (originally referred to as the EQG).\n\nIn summary, the G-Wagon is a **luxury, body-on-frame, military-derived off-road SUV** that serves as one of the ultimate automotive status symbols in the world.",
